WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.453
HTML, Office files, and tables.

2
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:15.555
HTML looks easier because the structure is explicit, but web and intranet pages wrap a little content in a lot of chrome: navigation, cookie banners, footers, related-article lists, scripts.

3
00:00:15.835 --> 00:00:26.565
Keep the main content, and keep the structure: convert headings to Markdown headings rather than flattening them, because headings become chunk boundaries and context in lesson 5.

4
00:00:26.845 --> 00:00:41.331
Libraries such as Trafilatura specialize in extracting the main text from web pages; for an intranet with one template, a few lines of parser code that skip nav, footer, script, and style are often enough, which is what the lab does.

5
00:00:41.611 --> 00:00:51.040
Office files (DOCX, PPTX, XLSX) are zipped XML with real structure, so layout-aware tools like Docling read them well.

6
00:00:51.320 --> 00:01:00.664
Two traps: slide decks put meaning in visual arrangement and speaker notes (decide whether notes are content), and spreadsheets are tables, not prose.

7
00:01:00.944 --> 00:01:12.997
A model given a raw CSV dump of a 5,000-row sheet will not retrieve well; a sheet is often better served by a SQL or tool lookup than by embedding.

8
00:01:13.277 --> 00:01:18.227
Tables deserve their own rule: never split a table across chunks without its header row.

9
00:01:18.507 --> 00:01:25.675
A row like “Unused, no receipt | 60 days | Store credit” is answerable; the same row without the header is a riddle.

10
00:01:25.955 --> 00:01:36.749
Common approaches are to convert each table to Markdown and keep it whole if it fits, or to emit one line per row with the header repeated (“Item type: Unused, no receipt.

11
00:01:37.029 --> 00:01:38.971
Return window: 60 days.

12
00:01:39.251 --> 00:01:41.128
Refund type: Store credit.

13
00:01:41.408 --> 00:01:41.738
”).

14
00:01:42.018 --> 00:01:48.098
Test with real questions about your tables, because which one works better depends on the embedding model.
